WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:06.690
The diver screams a silent scream as the giant
squid’s beak digs into his skin, its many

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:10.380
grasping tentacles grabbing him and holding
him in place.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.990
Nearby, his fellow divers are driven half-mad
with terror as they see mysterious figures

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:21.570
floating towards them through the murk - And
strange, Russian voices speaking in their

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:22.570
heads.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:23.670
Nobody can help them.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:28.390
They’re down too deep, too consumed by the
darkness and the pressure of the sea.

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:33.390
They’re now at the mercy of whatever is
inside the submarine…

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:38.220
Beginning in the 1940s with the dawn of nuclear
weapons, the American government conducting

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:43.370
the world’s first atomic weapons test, and
the Soviet Union responding in kind with nuclear

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:49.160
testing of their own, the two powers entered
an arms race fueled by rivalry and a thirst

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:52.380
to prove their strength on the world stage.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:57.760
What followed were decades of staggering technological
advancements as each nation tried to outdo

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.890
and intimidate the other.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:05.320
The Soviet Union crossed into the cold reaches
of outer space, deploying the satellite Sputnik.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:08.540
The United States responded with the founding
of NASA.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:14.270
Tensions reached a fever pitch during the
Cuban Missile Crisis of 1962, as United States

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:18.500
citizens feared they were teetering on the
brink of nuclear war.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:24.450
In July of 1968, the Soviet Union, the United
Kingdom, and the United States came together

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:29.470
to sign the Treaty on the Nonproliferation
of Nuclear Weapons, agreeing to abandon their

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:34.850
pursuit of increased nuclear power and turn
their focus to disarmament.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:39.090
But several months before the treaty was signed,
the Soviet government was dealing in weaponry

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:44.450
far more dangerous than nuclear missiles,
something the other world powers knew nothing

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:45.450
about.

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:50.260
These deadly secrets were hidden aboard a
submarine known as SCP-741.

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:57.920
SCP-741 is the underwater wreckage of a Soviet
submarine that sank in March 1968 and came

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.020
into the custody of the SCP Foundation in
1999.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:07.820
The submarine, a version of the Charlie II
class, was deployed under unusual circumstances,

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:11.090
which attracted the attention of the United
States government.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:16.060
In an attempt to learn more about the submarine,
the US Government launched Project [Redacted],

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:20.410
which attempted to recover the vessel from
the ocean in the early 1970s.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:25.810
They managed to recover a few pieces of the
vessel, though the specifics are highly classified.

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:32.170
In the late 1990s, the US Government contacted
the SCP Foundation, informing them of a possible

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:34.900
Euclid or Keter-class anomaly in the wreck.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:39.420
At this point, it passed into the custody,
and surveillance, of the Foundation.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:43.360
The submarine is on the ocean floor, broken
into three pieces.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:48.280
The hull was broken into bits during the Project
[Redacted] recovery attempt but was largely

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:54.170
intact when it first sank except for two holes,
one just in front of the sail and one just

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:56.430
below the starboard missile tubes.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:01.200
Apparently, the vessel sank rapidly due to
flooding after an enemy missile strike, which

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.250
occurred while it was surfacing.

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:08.350
Though all parts of the submarine are accounted
for, no members of the crew have been located.

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:13.440
Additionally, none of the emergency escape
equipment onboard has been used, raising further

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.400
questions about what became of the crew.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.250
Wherever they went, they seem to have left
something behind.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:24.860
Whenever divers are sent down to investigate
the wreckage, they report experiencing anomalous

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:31.420
currents and strange sea life, and hearing
moans, disembodied voices, and incoherent

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:32.420
whispering.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:35.760
They also report seeing blurry, faintly glowing
figures.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.780
Additionally, the ocean life in the area is
unnaturally aggressive, particularly large

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:42.610
squid and sharks.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:47.280
This effect was first noted during a manned
expedition into the waters around the wreckage,

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:51.220
described in Incident Report I741-A.

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:56.900
Divers A26 through A30 embarked from the icebreaker
Yamal into the waters below with the intention

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:01.250
of studying the potential anomalous activity
surrounding the wreckage.

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:05.420
As the group of divers approached the submarine,
they could feel the shift in the water around

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:10.329
them, the oppressive feeling of abnormally
increased pressure bearing down and threatening

00:04:10.329 --> 00:04:11.709
to crush them.

00:04:11.709 --> 00:04:16.340
If their specialized diving equipment were
to fail, they know that that would be it,

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:20.140
and the command would have to come and fish
their corpses out of the water.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:25.220
Or, even worse, they could vanish entirely,
meeting the same mysterious fate as the crew

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.060
of that doomed submarine.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:28.920
But they couldn't fixate on that.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.160
They had a job to do.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:36.450
At first glance, the wreckage appeared undisturbed,
unchanged since the previous inspection.

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:39.810
Still, they needed to take a closer look to
be sure.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:44.230
As the team got closer to the vessel, A30
was startled by the sight of movement from

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:48.710
within the wreckage, spotting motion through
one of the holes in the structure as something

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:49.860
passed by.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:55.050
He pointed it out to his colleagues, but they
dismissed this as likely a giant isopod or

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:58.650
spider crab, which were crawling all over
the surrounding area.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:03.590
A30 laughed off his jumpiness, agreed that
they were probably right, and continued the

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:05.720
exploration as planned.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:10.670
Control authorized the divers to proceed to
the next step, and A29 activated sonar and

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:13.560
lights, moving toward the starboard-side breach.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.360
There, a faint glowing caught his eye.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:21.050
The glow resembled that of radioactive material,
but when the team checked the radiation levels

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:23.759
in the area, they remained stable.

00:05:23.759 --> 00:05:27.400
Whatever the source of the glow was, it wasn’t
radioactive.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:32.039
At this point, his neutron counter began to
register something, and all of a sudden, a

00:05:32.039 --> 00:05:37.550
disembodied voice could be heard saying, "Vasily,
Yevgeny, can you hear me?"

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:41.630
The voice was muffled, and though it could
be heard over their communication channels,

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:44.470
it emanated from somewhere in the wreckage.

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:45.639
Deep, deep underwater.

00:05:45.639 --> 00:05:51.080
As A29 watched, a glowing shape emerged from
the darkness.

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:56.569
It was only an outline, the suggestion of
a silhouette, but the shape was undeniably

00:05:56.569 --> 00:05:57.569
familiar.

00:05:57.569 --> 00:05:59.639
It looked like a person.

00:05:59.639 --> 00:06:03.850
The other divers quickly noticed the apparition
too and began to panic.

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:08.180
Some checked their nitrogen levels, believing
it to be some sort of nitrogen narcosis, while

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:12.419
others pointed out that they couldn’t all
be suffering from nitrogen narcosis at the

00:06:12.419 --> 00:06:13.669
same time.

00:06:13.669 --> 00:06:18.599
The unknown voice continued speaking, saying
"Help me, it's getting hard to breathe."

00:06:18.599 --> 00:06:23.849
The divers debated what to do next, questioning
whether what they were seeing was even real,

00:06:23.849 --> 00:06:27.440
when all at once, the humanoid figure vanished
from sight.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:32.169
The divers attempted to shake off the startling
encounter, and they resumed their duties,

00:06:32.169 --> 00:06:34.600
the investigation proceeding as normal.

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:39.050
After a few moments of uneventful work, another
interruption presented itself in the form

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:42.690
of a five-meter-long squid swimming around
the wreckage.

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:47.290
Its presence startled the divers at first,
particularly those closest to the animal,

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:51.240
but the others encouraged them to ignore it,
citing the fact that there are no records

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.590
of these squid attacking humans.

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:58.840
So they did, continuing their work as the
squid circled them with apparent curiosity.

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:05.169
A27 spotted an unusual shell below the wreckage,
notably large and difficult to identify and

00:07:05.169 --> 00:07:09.389
control requested that they bring it up to
the surface for further inspection by a marine

00:07:09.389 --> 00:07:10.419
biologist.

00:07:10.419 --> 00:07:15.810
The divers began attaching haul cables to
the shell as the squid crept closer and closer.

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:20.919
All of a sudden, A26 screamed, and there was
a sudden bloom of blood in the water.

00:07:20.919 --> 00:07:26.820
The squid, despite it being uncharacteristic
behavior for its species, attacked the diver,

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:28.360
biting him savagely.

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:33.360
Prompted by cries for help from the diving
team, control began to reel A26 back toward

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:38.389
the surface, proceeding slowly to give him
decompression time, and the squid took advantage

00:07:38.389 --> 00:07:39.599
of the slow retreat.

00:07:39.599 --> 00:07:44.479
It chased after the diver, grabbing hold of
him and biting down again, tearing away at

00:07:44.479 --> 00:07:47.099
him as it gripped him in its tentacles.

00:07:47.099 --> 00:07:51.610
Still, Control continued reeling him in, hoping
to free him from the squid’s grasp as they

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:53.440
yanked him to the surface.

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:57.500
The other divers were ordered to get themselves
out of there as fast as possible, an order

00:07:57.500 --> 00:07:59.370
they gladly obeyed.

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:03.919
As they swam back to the surface, A30 saw
something else moving in the depths.

00:08:03.919 --> 00:08:08.150
He couldn't make out what it was, but the
sight of it gave him a sick feeling of dread

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:10.129
deep in the pit of his stomach.

00:08:10.129 --> 00:08:13.770
The four divers were recovered alive, along
with the shell.

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:18.800
Examination of the shell indicated that it
resembled that of the extinct orthoconic nautiloids,

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:20.580
but it was not fossilized.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:25.810
It was taken for further study, given the
possible implication of extinct species anomalously

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:30.160
manifesting in the vicinity of SCP-714.

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:35.560
Diver A26 lost a limb, and was exposed to
an unknown venom via bites from the attacking

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:36.560
squid.

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:38.730
His camera was destroyed in the process.

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:43.640
As squid are not known to attack humans unprovoked,
this behavior has been attributed to the influence

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:49.330
of SCP-741, though the exact link between
the two is yet to be determined.

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:55.280
An anomalous pressure gradient surrounds the
wreck with a radius of approximately 250m,

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:57.339
starting around the center of the submarine.

00:08:57.339 --> 00:09:01.420
The pressure in this area is much greater
than it should be, given the depth of the

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:02.430
waters there.

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:07.190
This unusually high pressure makes sonar analysis
extremely difficult, as well as threatening

00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:10.180
the safety of any divers in the vicinity of
the wreck.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:14.850
The few records that the Foundation has managed
to obtain from the Russian and U.S. governments

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:19.370
indicate that the submarine was being used
to transport some sort of secret cargo.

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:23.540
Though the specifics of this cargo are still
unknown, there is reason to believe that it

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:27.620
differed from any type of nuclear or chemical
weaponry.

00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:33.610
On a date redacted from official files, the
SCPS Basisty was patrolling the area around

00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:40.800
SCP-741 when its sonar detected some unknown
entity approaching SCP-741 from the South

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:43.040
at a pace of 46 knots.

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:47.940
The crew compared the acoustic signature of
the contact with known submarines and torpedoes

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:49.910
but could not find a match.

00:09:49.910 --> 00:09:55.240
The Basisty attempted to reach the contact
via sonobuoy drops and active sonar pings,

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:56.430
but it did not respond.

00:09:56.430 --> 00:10:01.820
When the contact crossed into the total underwater
exclusion zone, it became classified as hostile.

00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:06.910
At that point, the Sonar recorded sounds of
an undersea missile launch, and Basisty responded

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:08.910
with the utmost urgency.

00:10:08.910 --> 00:10:14.100
The ship broke away from its original area
and fired a Type 53 torpedo at the underwater

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:15.100
threat.

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:20.000
Fifteen seconds after the Basisty launched
its torpedo, missiles of an unidentified configuration

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:25.470
were seen breaking the water, flying at a
height of 1.8 meters and a velocity of 0.92

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:26.470
Mach.

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:30.910
The missiles did not emit any detectable radar,
nor did they respond to any launched chaff

00:10:30.910 --> 00:10:32.620
or flares from the Basisty.

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:39.270
Both of the missiles were engaged by Basisty's
3K95 Kinzhal surface-to-air missiles and Kashtan

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:45.540
point defense systems, and were destroyed
at 1800 meters and 210 meters from impact,

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:46.540
respectively.

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:50.839
After the missiles were neutralized, the hostile
vessel could be heard engaging in evasive

00:10:50.839 --> 00:10:51.839
maneuvers.

00:10:51.839 --> 00:10:57.360
At this point , there were four closely-spaced
explosions, and the sound of a submarine disintegrating.

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:03.120
The identity of the attacker, as well as its
intentions toward SCP-741, have not yet been

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:04.130
determined.

00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:08.580
The incident resulted in the Foundation research
team suggesting an expansion of the acoustic

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:13.440
sensor net, as well as additional patrol and
defense assets placed in the area.

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:17.339
Additionally, they advised an acquisition
of undersea retaliatory capability.

00:11:17.339 --> 00:11:21.839
The incident was classified Incident I741-C.

00:11:21.839 --> 00:11:28.150
The sonar recordings from the SCPS Basisty
during Incident I741-C were taken for further

00:11:28.150 --> 00:11:30.310
analysis by the research team.

00:11:30.310 --> 00:11:34.680
The in-depth review revealed anomalous acoustic
signatures that did not match up with any

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:39.120
known forms of propulsion, including magnetohydrodynamic
drive.

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:43.510
Currently, the nature of the unidentified
attacker remains a mystery, and it has not

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:47.540
been attributed to any particular government
or organization.

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:52.490
Following the incident involving the SCPS
Basisty, an American intelligence agent reached

00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:57.579
out to the Foundation, offering further insight
into the secretive government programs looking

00:11:57.579 --> 00:11:59.230
into SCP-741.

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:04.470
He agreed to sit for an interview with a Foundation
researcher assigned to the project, on the

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:07.310
condition that his identity remain protected.

00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:11.260
The SCP Researcher's name is absent from the
official file as well.

00:12:11.260 --> 00:12:15.650
The two men sat in a Foundation interview
room, and the interviewer asked his informant

00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:20.060
why he chose to come forward, given the US
Government had simply chosen to sit on this

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:21.870
information for 30 years.

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:25.139
"You've seen those reports - Project [REDACTED].

00:12:25.139 --> 00:12:26.139
We knew that part, too.

00:12:26.139 --> 00:12:29.100
How the directors didn't make the connection
is beyond me.

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:31.120
That, and the stuff the [Redacted] pulled
up?

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:32.120
Yeah.

00:12:32.120 --> 00:12:35.199
The other part you don't hear about is what
some of the research team died of.

00:12:35.199 --> 00:12:36.920
And the crewmen we buried?

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:38.430
Just uniforms.

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:44.410
Also, the nuclear device we recovered wasn't
a missile or torpedo warhead, it was a demolition

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:45.410
charge.

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:46.800
Does that make any sense?

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:48.650
After all those clues, I had to come forward.

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:52.180
Why the directorate didn't is something I
can't fully explain."

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:58.760
This particular statement puzzled the Foundation
Researcher, raising questions he hadn't anticipated.

00:12:58.760 --> 00:12:59.880
Only uniforms?

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:02.560
Did this mean that the sub had been unmanned?

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:06.160
The informant replied, "No, not unmanned.

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:10.690
There were no bodies, but personal effects
were everywhere, along with uniforms.

00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:15.640
There was some blood - human, before you ask
- on one of the torpedoes and a bit of skin,

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:19.019
where somebody probably crushed his hand loading
the thing.

00:13:19.019 --> 00:13:20.270
Just, no bodies left.

00:13:20.270 --> 00:13:24.430
When I first looked into all this, I had no
clue what the hell had gone on down there,

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:26.800
but I started putting things together."

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:30.529
The Foundation Agent began to speculate based
on the mounting evidence.

00:13:30.529 --> 00:13:32.840
Could it have been a Soviet Weapons Program?

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:35.430
A deadly biological agent of some kind?

00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:37.290
"No, no, it wasn't that.

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:42.130
I thought maybe it could have been, so I dialed
up some of my contacts at Biopreparat - us

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:47.110
spies wind up owing each other favors after
a while - and they denied it.

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:48.110
Vehemently.

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:51.829
Not your usual cover-up baloney, either; they
clearly stated that whatever the sub was carrying,

00:13:51.829 --> 00:13:55.110
it wasn't theirs, and they wanted no part
of it.

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:57.420
Sounded like he was gonna puke when I mentioned
[REDACTED].

00:13:57.420 --> 00:14:02.670
And Doctor, do you have any idea what it takes
to make a bioweapons researcher sick?"

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:05.440
Now, that wasn't what really bugged me, though.

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:11.120
What really kept me awake at night was the
KGB files that fell into our hands.

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:16.230
They mentioned a covert op by the Soviet military
against an internal, unnamed faction, to get

00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:22.579
rid of "terrifying weapons" that "even the
Soviet Union can't safely control".

00:14:22.579 --> 00:14:27.060
They wanted to lose it, whatever it was, or
maybe fob it off onto the US.

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:31.130
Of course, that all came to light right before
the Iron Curtain fell, and given the atmosphere

00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:35.120
at the time, it was practically impossible
to convince the directors that they weren't

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:39.860
talking about nukes, and even once I did,
they still didn't think this was worthy of

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:40.860
action.

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:45.120
I mean, the [REDACTED] will probably have
me hanged for treason if they ever find me,

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:46.520
but it was worth the risk.

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:51.440
And by what I can gather, it sounds like Russia
thinks so too - loaning you half the Pacific

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:52.630
Fleet, and all…"

00:14:52.630 --> 00:14:56.699
The interview continued after this point,
but the rest of the conversation was considered

00:14:56.699 --> 00:14:59.399
irrelevant and stricken from the official
file.

00:14:59.399 --> 00:15:03.459
The interview left the Foundation with more
questions than answers, though they were more

00:15:03.459 --> 00:15:09.290
certain than ever that SCP-741 must be kept
under strict containment procedures.

00:15:09.290 --> 00:15:13.410
Due to the object's location at the bottom
of the sea, as well as the unusually elevated

00:15:13.410 --> 00:15:18.529
pressure around it, it is unlikely that many
civilians will come into contact with it.

00:15:18.529 --> 00:15:23.220
However, as an extra protective measure, sonar
and submersible monitoring is conducted on

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:28.220
a periodic basis in order to verify that the
wreckage has not been interfered with in any

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:29.220
way.

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:34.540
The Foundation-contracted Russian warships,
SCPS Basisty and Krasnoyarsk, have been selected

00:15:34.540 --> 00:15:35.779
for this purpose.

00:15:35.779 --> 00:15:41.780
If any unauthorized activity occurs in the
area surrounding SCP-741, nuclear and conventional

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:43.509
missiles may be deployed.

00:15:43.509 --> 00:15:48.310
Any movement of SCP-741 is grounds for an
immediate nuclear strike.

00:15:48.310 --> 00:15:53.920
Whatever secrets SCP-741 holds, whatever it
was transporting that was even more of an

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:58.440
uncontrollable threat than nuclear warfare,
they're better left alone down there at the

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:00.230
bottom of the sea.

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:05.440
Now check out “SCP-2316 - The Bodies in
the Water” and “SCP-054 - Water Nymph”

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:06.149
for more!

